config WTK_ILITEK_E977
	bool "ILITEK touch for the MediaTek package (Doogee S88 Pro, from the disassembly)"
	default n
	help
	  ILITEK touchscreen of the Doogee S88 Pro, reconstructed by reading the
	  disassembled factory kernel.  The factory config turns it on
	  (CONFIG_WTK_ILITEK_E977=y) and the ALPS tree does not have the symbol:
	  without this entry `olddefconfig` discards the factory config line in
	  silence.

	  The factory block has 153 functions in eight translation units.

	  THERE IS NO CONSTRAINT WITH THE OTHER TWO TOUCHSCREENS, and an earlier
	  draft of this Kconfig had a wrong one.  It said the three exclude each
	  other, and chained them with `depends on`; the factory config turns ALL
	  THREE on:

	    2220:CONFIG_WTK_GT917S_E977=y
	    2224:CONFIG_WTK_ILITEK_E977=y
	    2227:CONFIG_WTK_FT8719_E977=y

	  And it makes sense: MediaTek's `tpd` framework registers several drivers
	  and chooses at run time which panel answers.  This driver's `tpd_probe`,
	  `tpd_suspend`, `tpd_resume` and `tpd_local_init` are `static`, so they do
	  not collide with those of FT8719 and GT917S -- the three do not even have
	  one global symbol in common, and it is measured:

	    $ comm -12 <(nm --defined-only --extern-only GT917S/built-in.o) \
	                <(nm --defined-only --extern-only ilitek_e977/built-in.o)
	    (no lines)

	  But all three register a `tpd_driver_t` for the same panel, and only one
	  is to be turned on.
